 "transcript": {
  "answer": "**Short answer:** Women in Victoria are in work in record-level numbers, but their unemployment rate has risen over the past year, and it's now slightly above men's. These are seasonally adjusted monthly figures, so single months are noisy.\n\n**Latest (September 2026), women in Victoria:**\n- **Unemployment rate: 5.1%.** This is up from 4.1% in September 2025 and 4.6% in October 2024. It peaked at 5.2% in July 2026 and has eased slightly since.\n- **Participation rate: 63.6%.** This is roughly where it was a year ago (63.9% in September 2025).\n- **Employed: about 1,843,000.** This is up about 68,000 since October 2024 and roughly flat over the past year.\n- **Full-time: about 1,008,000.** This is up about 39,000 since October 2024, with most of the gain coming in 2025.\n- **Part-time: about 835,000** (calculated as total employed minus full-time). This is up from about 828,000 a year earlier.\n\n**Comparison with men in Victoria (September 2026):**\n- Unemployment rate: 5.0%, down from 5.3% a year earlier.\n- Participation rate: 71.5%, so women's participation is about 8 points lower.\n\n**What it means:** Women's employment has grown, but the jobs gain has been concentrated in full-time work in 2025, and since then the gains have been modest. Their unemployment rate has risen about a point over the year while men's has fallen, so women are now slightly more likely than men to be unemployed. Whether that's a lasting shift or month-to-month noise isn't clear from one year of data.",
